---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Visitors — Northern Light Initiative" description="How research visits with Northern Light members work: casual visits, the Dunlap and CITA visitor programs, CITA MoU exchanges, Mitacs Globalink, and the initiative's own program in preparation.">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="kicker" style="color:#84e6ca;">Visitors</div>
      <h1>Visiting Toronto</h1>
      <p>How research visits with Northern Light members usually work, and how to arrange one.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:40px;">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:880px;">

      <div class="tier">
        <h2>How visits work</h2>
        <p style="max-width:none;">
          Casual visits are always welcome. If you are passing through Toronto, write to the
          Northern Light member closest to your research and come by for a talk or a day of
          discussions. The programs below go further, funding a stay or giving it formal status,
          and they run through a Toronto host rather than an open application: your host handles
          the internal side and knows the current timing, so the first step is the same e-mail.
          Funded visits in recent years have run from about a week to a few months. Visitors are
          announced within the departments, usually give a talk, and are welcome at the group
          meetings and colloquia across the member institutes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="tier" id="dunlap" style="margin-top:44px;">
        <h2>Dunlap Visitor Program</h2>
        <p style="max-width:none;">
          The Dunlap Institute funds research visits hosted by its members, and Northern Light
          members can co-host visitors through the program. Each visit is tied to a named Dunlap
          host, so the way in is to contact your prospective host first. The institute keeps a
          public list of past visitors; it is not always current (when we last checked, it ended in
          late 2024), but it gives a good sense of the length and variety of visits.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:12px;">
          <a class="link-more" href="https://www.dunlap.utoronto.ca/people/visitors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Past visitors at Dunlap <i class="ti ti-external-link"></i></a>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="tier" id="cita" style="margin-top:44px;">
        <h2>CITA Visitor Program</h2>
        <p style="max-width:none;">
          CITA hosts visiting scientists in theoretical astrophysics, from short stays to longer
          ones. Here too the visit is arranged with a host, so the first step is an e-mail to the
          member you would like to work with. CITA's site lists current visitors and the practical
          details of a visit, from travel to reimbursement.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:12px; display:flex; gap:22px; flex-wrap:wrap;">
          <a class="link-more" href="https://www.cita.utoronto.ca/people/current-visitors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Current visitors at CITA <i class="ti ti-external-link"></i></a>
          <a class="link-more" href="https://www.cita.utoronto.ca/visitor-info/getting-here/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Practical information <i class="ti ti-external-link"></i></a>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="tier" id="mou" style="margin-top:44px;">
        <h2>CITA Memoranda of Understanding</h2>
        <p style="max-width:none;">
          CITA holds agreements (MoUs) with partner institutions that support exchange visits in
          both directions. If your institute is among the partners, an MoU visit is often the
          simplest funded route, arranged with a CITA host as above. Summaries of the current
          agreements are on CITA's site.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:12px;">
          <a class="link-more" href="https://www.cita.utoronto.ca/opportunities/mou-summaries/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CITA MoU summaries <i class="ti ti-external-link"></i></a>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="tier" id="mitacs" style="margin-top:44px;">
        <h2>Mitacs Globalink</h2>
        <p style="max-width:none;">
          Mitacs Globalink funds researchers from abroad at Canadian universities, including U of T
          and York. The Globalink Research Award supports graduate students and postdoctoral
          fellows for projects of 12 to 48 weeks under joint home and host supervision (CA$6,000
          per 12 to 24 week unit), and the Globalink Research Internship brings international
          undergraduates for 12-week summer projects. Northern Light members can host through both
          programs: agree on a project with your host first, then apply through Mitacs.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:12px;">
          <a class="link-more" href="https://www.mitacs.ca/our-programs/globalink-research-award/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Globalink Research Award <i class="ti ti-external-link"></i></a>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="tier" id="northernlight" style="margin-top:44px;">
        <h2>Northern Light Visitor Program</h2>
        <p style="max-width:none;">
          Northern Light is preparing a visitor program of its own; terms will be announced here.
          Until it opens, the routes above are the way to visit, and the coordinators are happy to
          advise on which one fits your plans.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="tier" style="margin-top:44px;">
        <h2>Not sure whom to write to?</h2>
        <p style="max-width:none;">
          See <a href="/people">People</a> for members and their research areas, or reach the
          coordinators through the contact address in the footer. We are happy to match a visit
          idea with the right host.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
